  "orderInScreen": 6,
  "surface": "backend",
  "riskClass": "medium",
  "title": "inventory-service transaction route accepts any transactionType string without enum validation",
  "description": "R6 deep audit finding. Dimension: input_validation. Severity: P2. Source SHA: bbd487a3. Scope: R6_DEEP_AUDIT_WAVE. The POST /stores/:storeId/inventory/transactions route (lines 97-128) accepts `body.transactionType` directly from request body and passes it to `createTransaction` without validating it against the allowed enum values ('sale', 'sale_return', 'purchase_received', 'adjustment', 'opening_stock'). The TypeScript type `TransactionType` only provides compile-time checking, not runtime validation. An attacker could send `transactionType: 'foo'` which would either: (1) be inserted into the DB if the column has no CHECK constraint, creating invalid ledger data, or (2) cause a DB error that propagates as a 500 instead of a clean 400 validation error. Similarly, `referenceType` is not validated at the route level.",
  "status": "done",
  "severity": "P2",
